WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.450
Welcome to Module 7 of the Provider
Notices learning path: routing to owners.

2
00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:08.888
A notice nobody owns is the
problem this path started with.

3
00:00:08.888 --> 00:00:12.562
This module is where each notice
that needs action gets an owner.

4
00:00:12.562 --> 00:00:16.137
It's Gate 6 of the provider
notices implementation guide,

5
00:00:16.137 --> 00:00:18.724
with Chapter 6 of the developer workbook.

6
00:00:18.724 --> 00:00:23.897
Each notice that needs action becomes a
typed task in the service desk's front door,

7
00:00:23.897 --> 00:00:28.612
with a severity, a response window, and
the provider's own date kept beside it.

8
00:00:28.612 --> 00:00:30.838
The rest go in the weekly digest.

9
00:00:30.838 --> 00:00:33.618
In this module you'll
check the lab's routing,

10
00:00:33.618 --> 00:00:37.558
route two fictional weeks from
all five providers as a dry run,

11
00:00:37.558 --> 00:00:41.112
read one payload in full, and
watch a re-run write nothing.

12
00:00:41.112 --> 00:00:43.343
It takes about sixty-five minutes,

13
00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:47.724
with a lab on fixtures and a lab in
a ServiceNow developer instance.

14
00:00:47.724 --> 00:00:53.680
The code is illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not run against a live instance.

15
00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:58.742
Why does routing matter?

16
00:00:58.742 --> 00:00:59.880
Three reasons.

17
00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.380
First, a notice carries
no owner and no due date.

18
00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:07.197
Routing is where each notice
that needs action gets both:

19
00:01:07.197 --> 00:01:11.257
a typed task in the front door,
not a second queue, with an owner,

20
00:01:11.257 --> 00:01:14.993
a severity, a response window,
and the provider's own date.

21
00:01:14.993 --> 00:01:17.656
Second, providers repeat themselves.

22
00:01:17.656 --> 00:01:20.830
The same notice comes back
in every weekly read.

23
00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:23.855
A router that ticketed it every
week would fill the queue,

24
00:01:23.855 --> 00:01:25.593
and people would stop reading it.

25
00:01:25.593 --> 00:01:27.859
So the router remembers
what it has routed,

26
00:01:27.859 --> 00:01:30.579
and writes nothing for a
notice that hasn't changed.

27
00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:35.242
Third, a provider closing its notice
isn't the agency's work being done.

28
00:01:35.242 --> 00:01:37.993
A retirement can close
on the provider's side

29
00:01:37.993 --> 00:01:40.967
while the agency still
runs the retired version.

30
00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:46.456
So a task closes only when the agency's own
next collection shows the notice closed,

31
00:01:46.456 --> 00:01:49.442
or the agency's resources
no longer affected.

32
00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:50.980
That's the re-read.

33
00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:57.643
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

34
00:01:57.643 --> 00:02:01.267
You'll write routing rules in the
right order, and say why security

35
00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:05.429
advisories and retirements come before
the general action-required rule.

36
00:02:05.429 --> 00:02:08.179
You'll route a collection as a dry run,

37
00:02:08.179 --> 00:02:12.393
and read what each payload is
for: create, update or close.

38
00:02:12.393 --> 00:02:17.209
You'll read a task payload: its
severity, priority and response window,

39
00:02:17.209 --> 00:02:22.279
the provider's own date, the contingency
review flag and the five clauses.

40
00:02:22.279 --> 00:02:24.808
You'll explain why a
re-run writes nothing,

41
00:02:24.808 --> 00:02:29.418
and why a provider's closure asks for
the re-read instead of closing the task.

42
00:02:29.418 --> 00:02:33.042
And you'll say where the agency's
routing is written and kept.

43
00:02:33.042 --> 00:02:36.349
Before you start, finish at
least one of Modules 2 to 6,

44
00:02:36.349 --> 00:02:40.502
and read the Ticket-to-Machine
reference appendix in Governance,

45
00:02:40.502 --> 00:02:45.655
Drift and Evidence, for the typed task, its
lanes and the five-clause request chain.

46
00:02:45.655 --> 00:02:49.817
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1
or later with Pester 5.

47
00:02:49.817 --> 00:02:51.090
No tenant.

48
00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.940
The agency's routing is a
JSON file of ordered rules.

49
00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:04.715
Each rule has an I D, a match, and
a route: task, digest or ignore.

50
00:03:04.715 --> 00:03:09.691
A match can name providers, kinds and
statuses; whether action is required

51
00:03:09.691 --> 00:03:14.340
or the notice names resources; and
patterns for services and regions.

52
00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.851
A task rule also carries a severity,
P1 to P4, an assignment group,

53
00:03:18.851 --> 00:03:23.102
an approver role, and whether
it needs a contingency review.

54
00:03:23.102 --> 00:03:28.049
The kit's routing check refuses an unknown
match key, any route other than the

55
00:03:28.049 --> 00:03:31.583
three, a task without a severity
or an assignment group,

56
00:03:31.583 --> 00:03:33.389
and any placeholder left in.

57
00:03:33.389 --> 00:03:34.415
Then the order.

58
00:03:34.415 --> 00:03:37.513
Rules are tried in order,
and the first match wins,

59
00:03:37.513 --> 00:03:42.160
so the specific rules come first:
security advisories, then retirements,

60
00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.491
then maintenance that names
the agency's resources,

61
00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:50.603
then open incidents that name them, then
anything else marked as needing action.

62
00:03:50.603 --> 00:03:51.852
The order matters.

63
00:03:51.852 --> 00:03:56.537
A retirement is often also marked action
required, and the retirement rule,

64
00:03:56.537 --> 00:04:00.610
which carries the contingency
review, should take it first.

65
00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:07.160
Each task is a ServiceNow-shaped payload.

66
00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:09.763
Its action is create, update or close,

67
00:04:09.763 --> 00:04:14.535
and its de-duplication key is the
provider and the provider's own I D.

68
00:04:14.535 --> 00:04:20.698
Its severity, priority and response window
come from the Ticket-to-Machine finding

69
00:04:20.698 --> 00:04:26.235
contract: P1 immediate, P2 one hour, P3
twenty-four hours, P4 seventy-two hours.

70
00:04:26.235 --> 00:04:30.052
Those windows are program design,
not a federal requirement,

71
00:04:30.052 --> 00:04:33.335
and a test reads the contract
and fails if they drift.

72
00:04:33.335 --> 00:04:36.960
Then the provider's own action
date, kept in a separate field.

73
00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.310
The two dates answer different questions.

74
00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:43.422
The response window says how fast
the owner must pick the task up.

75
00:04:43.422 --> 00:04:46.333
The provider's date says
when the work must be done,

76
00:04:46.333 --> 00:04:48.747
and for a retirement
it may be months away.

77
00:04:48.747 --> 00:04:54.323
Confusing them makes a P3 task look due
in a year, or a retirement due tomorrow.

78
00:04:54.323 --> 00:04:59.009
The payload also carries the contingency
review flag, and the Ticket-to-Machine

79
00:04:59.009 --> 00:05:03.198
block: the Human decision lane, and
the five clauses of the request chain.

80
00:05:03.198 --> 00:05:06.192
Where it came from, who
approves, what to do,

81
00:05:06.192 --> 00:05:09.360
the re-read check, and the evidence kept.

82
00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:14.285
Runs and state.

83
00:05:14.285 --> 00:05:18.296
The router compares each notice with
its state, which is keyed by the

84
00:05:18.296 --> 00:05:22.235
provider and the provider's I D and
holds each notice's fingerprint.

85
00:05:22.235 --> 00:05:25.498
A task-routed notice gets a
create the first time it's open.

86
00:05:25.498 --> 00:05:27.370
It gets an update when it changes,

87
00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:30.698
meaning a different fingerprint,
or when its rule changes.

88
00:05:30.698 --> 00:05:32.809
An unchanged notice writes nothing.

89
00:05:32.809 --> 00:05:37.284
When the provider closes its notice,
the task gets a close payload,

90
00:05:37.284 --> 00:05:41.923
but that close asks for the re-read,
clause C4; it doesn't close the task.

91
00:05:41.923 --> 00:05:44.760
The task is resolved on
the agency's own check.

92
00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:49.228
And an open notice from a provider read
in this run that's no longer listed

93
00:05:49.228 --> 00:05:53.547
is marked absent, and its task gets an
update asking for the same re-read.

94
00:05:53.547 --> 00:05:57.947
A provider that wasn't read this run
never has its notices marked absent.

95
00:05:57.947 --> 00:06:00.738
Each run writes the payloads, a task list,

96
00:06:00.738 --> 00:06:04.373
a routing spreadsheet file,
the state and a manifest.

97
00:06:04.373 --> 00:06:09.030
It's a dry run: nothing is sent unless
the scheduled run's send switch is given,

98
00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:11.800
with sending turned on in the settings.

99
00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:16.275
Try it.

100
00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:21.525
Lab 1 runs in the provider notices
folder of the examples, in PowerShell 7.

101
00:06:21.525 --> 00:06:24.300
The teams in the lab's
routing are fictional.

102
00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:29.225
Step one checks the lab's routing:
six rules, in the order they're tried.

103
00:06:29.225 --> 00:06:33.957
Five route to a task: security
operations at P2, cloud platform

104
00:06:33.957 --> 00:06:39.525
engineering at P3 twice, the service
desk at P2, and cloud operations at P3.

105
00:06:39.525 --> 00:06:40.987
The sixth ignores.

106
00:06:40.987 --> 00:06:43.373
Step two tries the template as it ships,

107
00:06:43.373 --> 00:06:46.876
and the kit refuses it, because
it still has a placeholder.

108
00:06:46.876 --> 00:06:50.813
It runs only once every
value is filled in, at work.

109
00:06:50.813 --> 00:06:55.053
Step three collects every provider
from the day-one fixtures:

110
00:06:55.053 --> 00:06:57.299
twenty-two notices, as in Module 1.

111
00:06:57.299 --> 00:06:59.762
Step four routes them as a dry run.

112
00:06:59.762 --> 00:07:01.501
Twenty-two new notices.

113
00:07:01.501 --> 00:07:05.758
Twelve route to a task,
nine go to the digest only,

114
00:07:05.758 --> 00:07:10.562
and one is ignored: the
Microsoft Viva post, by rule R06.

115
00:07:10.562 --> 00:07:15.250
That's twelve create payloads,
written to a folder, and nothing sent.

116
00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:24.188
Step five reads one payload in full:
the Azure retirement from Module 1.

117
00:07:24.188 --> 00:07:28.149
It's a create, keyed on Azure
and the notice's tracking I D.

118
00:07:28.149 --> 00:07:34.050
Its severity is P3, with a response window
of eighty-six thousand four hundred

119
00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:36.078
seconds, twenty-four hours,

120
00:07:36.078 --> 00:07:40.687
so the response is due November
tenth, 2026, a day after the run.

121
00:07:40.687 --> 00:07:43.356
The retirement date is kept apart,

122
00:07:43.356 --> 00:07:47.263
in the action due field:
September thirtieth, 2027.

123
00:07:47.263 --> 00:07:48.212
Both are right.

124
00:07:48.212 --> 00:07:50.622
The owner picks the task up within a day,

125
00:07:50.622 --> 00:07:54.237
and the work is due by the
retirement date, ten months later.

126
00:07:54.237 --> 00:08:00.309
It goes to cloud platform engineering,
with the contingency review flag set,

127
00:08:00.309 --> 00:08:04.163
and it supports C M three, C
M four, C P two and S I five.

128
00:08:04.163 --> 00:08:08.103
And the Ticket-to-Machine block
puts it in the Human decision lane,

129
00:08:08.103 --> 00:08:12.846
with the system owner as approver, and a
re-read check: the next collection shows

130
00:08:12.846 --> 00:08:18.030
the notice closed or no longer listed, or
the named resources no longer affected.

131
00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:25.842
Step six routes the same collection
again, on a copy of the state.

132
00:08:25.842 --> 00:08:30.337
All twenty-two notices are unchanged,
and the router writes nothing:

133
00:08:30.337 --> 00:08:34.030
no payloads, because nothing
new or changed needs a task.

134
00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:37.755
That's the guard against the same
notice being ticketed every week.

135
00:08:37.755 --> 00:08:40.693
Step seven collects and
routes the second week.

136
00:08:40.693 --> 00:08:44.435
Two new notices, six
changed, fifteen unchanged,

137
00:08:44.435 --> 00:08:49.329
and one no longer listed: one
create, four updates and one close.

138
00:08:49.329 --> 00:08:51.980
Step eight reads what each payload is for.

139
00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:55.967
The Azure and Oracle
rescheduled maintenance windows,

140
00:08:55.967 --> 00:08:59.955
and the updated Microsoft 365
post, are changed updates.

141
00:08:59.955 --> 00:09:03.003
The Azure incident the
provider no longer lists

142
00:09:03.003 --> 00:09:05.593
is an absent update,
asking for the re-read.

143
00:09:05.593 --> 00:09:09.467
The new Microsoft 365 post is a create.

144
00:09:09.467 --> 00:09:13.912
And the AWS security notification
that AWS closed is a close,

145
00:09:13.912 --> 00:09:18.180
which also asks for the re-read,
not for the task to be closed.

146
00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:20.438
Step nine runs the router's tests,

147
00:09:20.438 --> 00:09:25.705
including the one that reads the finding
contract: thirty-one pass, none fail.

148
00:09:25.705 --> 00:09:27.910
Then delete the output folder.

149
00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:35.610
Lab 2 sends one payload to a
ServiceNow developer instance.

150
00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:39.554
You need a Personal Developer Instance
or a sub-production instance,

151
00:09:39.554 --> 00:09:43.360
never production, with an endpoint
you build to accept the payload.

152
00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:46.760
Its shape is the agency's,
and not yet specified.

153
00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:49.972
You also need a token for
a test integration user,

154
00:09:49.972 --> 00:09:52.622
and Lab 1's steps three
and four run first.

155
00:09:52.622 --> 00:09:56.153
Put the token in the environment
for this session only,

156
00:09:56.153 --> 00:10:00.547
never in a file: the kit refuses
secret-like values in a settings file.

157
00:10:00.547 --> 00:10:05.321
Send one payload through the kit's sender,
and check the record's assignment group,

158
00:10:05.321 --> 00:10:08.598
priority, description and
five clauses in the instance.

159
00:10:08.598 --> 00:10:13.647
Then back it out: delete the test record,
clear the token, and delete the output.

160
00:10:13.647 --> 00:10:16.827
Until the endpoint exists,
the service desk works

161
00:10:16.827 --> 00:10:19.773
from the dry run's task
folder and routing file.

162
00:10:19.773 --> 00:10:24.421
And the agency's own routing is written
at work, from the kit's template,

163
00:10:24.421 --> 00:10:29.070
with its Copilot prompts in Microsoft
365 Copilot Chat if that helps,

164
00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:31.598
and kept inside the agency's boundary.

165
00:10:31.598 --> 00:10:34.120
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

166
00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:39.257
The federal requirement.

167
00:10:39.257 --> 00:10:44.377
Gate 6 supports NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three S I five,

168
00:10:44.377 --> 00:10:48.439
parts c and d: disseminating
advisories to the people who act,

169
00:10:48.439 --> 00:10:52.583
and implementing directives in
the established time frames.

170
00:10:52.583 --> 00:10:56.139
It supports C M three,
parts b and f, and C M four:

171
00:10:56.139 --> 00:11:02.857
a change a provider announces is reviewed,
and its impact analyzed, before it lands.

172
00:11:02.857 --> 00:11:06.831
And it supports C P two, part
e: retirements and maintenance

173
00:11:06.831 --> 00:11:11.383
that name the agency's resources
prompt a contingency plan review.

174
00:11:11.383 --> 00:11:15.786
The evidence is the routing check on
the agency's file; the task payloads,

175
00:11:15.786 --> 00:11:19.443
the task list and the routing
file; the payloads for changes,

176
00:11:19.443 --> 00:11:24.444
retirements and maintenance, and the change
records they open; the payloads with

177
00:11:24.444 --> 00:11:29.295
the contingency review flag; and the state,
which shows each notice routed once.

178
00:11:29.295 --> 00:11:34.357
No K S I cites base C M four;
two cite its second enhancement.

179
00:11:34.357 --> 00:11:38.395
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers,

180
00:11:38.395 --> 00:11:42.358
and which file supports which
indicator is the program's mapping.

181
00:11:42.358 --> 00:11:46.110
The assessor decides whether
each control is satisfied.

182
00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:51.835
Check yourself with two questions.

183
00:11:51.835 --> 00:11:55.104
First: a task's response
due date is tomorrow,

184
00:11:55.104 --> 00:11:58.010
and its action due date is in ten months.

185
00:11:58.010 --> 00:11:58.935
Which is right?

186
00:11:58.935 --> 00:12:00.610
The answer is b, both.

187
00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:04.472
The response window says how fast
the owner must pick the task up;

188
00:12:04.472 --> 00:12:07.460
the provider's date says
when the work must be done.

189
00:12:07.460 --> 00:12:11.447
They answer different questions, so
the kit keeps them in separate fields.

190
00:12:11.447 --> 00:12:15.397
Second: AWS closes its
security notification.

191
00:12:15.397 --> 00:12:16.685
What does the router do?

192
00:12:16.685 --> 00:12:17.897
The answer is c.

193
00:12:17.897 --> 00:12:21.300
It writes a close payload
that asks for the re-read,

194
00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:25.285
clause C4, and the task is resolved
on the agency's own check.

195
00:12:25.285 --> 00:12:29.235
The provider closing its notice
isn't the agency's work being done.

196
00:12:29.235 --> 00:12:31.662
The module page has three more questions,

197
00:12:31.662 --> 00:12:34.510
each linked to the
section that answers it.

198
00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:39.085
To sum up.

199
00:12:39.085 --> 00:12:42.812
You checked six routing rules
and saw the template refused,

200
00:12:42.812 --> 00:12:46.159
routed twenty-two notices
into twelve task payloads,

201
00:12:46.159 --> 00:12:51.103
read one with both its dates and its five
clauses, watched a re-run write nothing,

202
00:12:51.103 --> 00:12:54.298
and read the second week's
create, updates and close.

203
00:12:54.298 --> 00:13:00.014
The evidence an ISSO keeps for Gate 6
is each routing run's task payloads,

204
00:13:00.014 --> 00:13:04.180
task list, routing file,
state and manifest, all dated;

205
00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:10.285
the reviewed routing file's SHA-256; and
the change records the payloads open.

206
00:13:10.285 --> 00:13:13.360
Before you ship, work through
the chapter's checklist.

207
00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:18.085
Next is Module 8: digest,
evidence and go/no-go.

208
00:13:18.085 --> 00:13:23.189
That's Gate 7: the weekly digest the owners
read for everything that isn't a task,

209
00:13:23.189 --> 00:13:26.760
and the hashed evidence
chain that records every run.
